---
import time

# A real job runs somewhere else (a queue worker, an AI provider) and writes
# its progress to a store every instance can read: a database, KV or Redis.
# This page can't keep the job in its own process: serverless hosts such as
# Cloudflare Workers stop running your code once the response is sent, and
# the next request may reach a different instance. So the demo stands in for
# the store with the clock: the job "takes" JOB_SECONDS from its start time.
JOB_SECONDS = 2.0

status = wire("idle")
progress = wire(0)
started_at = wire(0.0)

# Literal syntax samples live in frontmatter: .wire prose cannot contain raw
# braces or a stray dollar sign -- the template parser would eat them.
POLL_SYNTAX = "@poll.every-400={tick()}"
STOP_SYNTAX = "{$if status == 'running'} ... {/if}"

def start():
    started_at.value = time.time()
    progress.value = 0
    status.value = "running"

def tick():
    # Each poll tick READS the job's progress into wires; that is the whole
    # channel from background work back to the page.
    elapsed = time.time() - started_at.value
    progress.value = min(100, int(elapsed / JOB_SECONDS * 5) * 20)
    if progress.value >= 100:
        status.value = "done"
---
<h1>Background jobs without a WebSocket</h1>
<p>A stateless page can't be pushed to, so it asks. Start a job, then a
polled element asks the server for the job's progress every 400&#8239;ms
until it finishes.</p>

<section id="job">
    <button id="start-btn" @click={start()}>Start generation</button>
    <p>Status: <span id="job-status">{status}</span></p>

    {$if status == 'running'}
    <div id="job-running">
        <p id="job-progress" @poll.every-400={tick()}>Generating&hellip; {progress}%</p>
        <p>The line above is the poll: <code>{POLL_SYNTAX}</code>. Every
        400&#8239;ms it sends one POST, exactly like a click. If the previous
        answer hasn't arrived yet, it skips that tick instead of piling up
        requests.</p>
    </div>
    {/if}

    {$if status == 'done'}
    <div id="job-done">
        <p>Done: {progress}%. The polled element is gone from the page, and
        its timer with it. The stop condition is the conditional render
        <code>{STOP_SYNTAX}</code>.</p>
        <button id="run-again" @click={start()}>Run again</button>
    </div>
    {/if}
</section>

<section id="why">
    <h2>Where does the progress live?</h2>
    <p>Not in the page's process. Serverless hosts such as Cloudflare Workers
    stop running your code once the response is sent, and the next poll may
    reach a different instance. A real job writes its progress to a store
    every instance can read (a database, KV or Redis), and <code>tick()</code>
    reads it from there. This demo computes the progress from the job's start
    time instead, so it needs no store.</p>
    <p>Polling suits bounded waits where a progress number every few hundred
    milliseconds is enough. Streaming an AI answer token by token needs server
    push: the WebSocket tier today.</p>
</section>
